            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Paul Cézanne</strong><br>\nPaul Cézanne was born on January 19, 1839, in Aix-en-Provence, France. His father was a wealthy banker, which allowed Cézanne to receive a good education and pursue his interest in art. Despite his father's wishes for him to attend law school, Cézanne enrolled at the Academy of Design in Aix in 1859. His early works were influenced by romanticism, but he later developed a more personal style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to Paris</strong><br>\nIn 1861, Cézanne moved to Paris, where he met and was influenced by Impressionist artists. He initially struggled to gain acceptance in the artistic community and was repeatedly rejected by the Paris Salon. This rejection fueled his determination to develop a new approach to art. Cézanne's friendship with Camille Pissarro, an Impressionist painter, was particularly influential.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Unique Artistic Vision</strong><br>\nCézanne's unique vision was to capture the essence of his subjects through color, line, and form. He often said that he wanted to make of Impressionism something solid and durable like the art of museums. His aim was to simplify nature into geometric forms such as cylinders, spheres, and cones. This method laid the groundwork for modern art movements like Cubism.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
