            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Card Players Series</strong><br>\nOne of Cézanne's most famous works is \"The Card Players,\" a series of paintings created in the early 1890s. These works depict Provencal peasants immersed in their game. The series showcases Cézanne's masterful use of shape and color to create depth and character, moving away from traditional perspectives and embracing a more abstract style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mont Sainte-Victoire Paintings</strong><br>\nCézanne painted Mont Sainte-Victoire, a mountain in southern France, more than sixty times during his career. These paintings are celebrated for their portrayal of the changing colors and light of the landscape. Through this repetition, Cézanne explored different viewpoints and atmospheric conditions, which reflected his evolving artistic approach.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Other Artists</strong><br>\nCézanne's work had a profound impact on the art world and was a significant influence on later generations of artists. His focus on the underlying structure of objects inspired artists like Picasso and Matisse. They admired his use of color and his approach to nature, which broke away from the traditional painting of the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
