          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Cézanne's Technique</strong><br>\nCézanne was known for applying paint in a dense, layered manner using a palette knife or brush. He built up surfaces with patches of color, creating a complex field of vision. This technique gave his paintings a robust and dynamic quality, making them appear more vivid and tactile.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Bathers Series</strong><br>\nThe Bathers is a series of paintings that Cézanne worked on for the last two decades of his life. It includes dozens of male figures in a landscape, displaying his ongoing interest in the human form and its relationship to the environment. These paintings are considered some of his most important works and embody the transitional period between traditional and modern art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nCézanne's last years were marked by recognition and increasing isolation. He died on October 22, 1906, after being caught in a storm while painting outdoors. By the time of his death, he was beginning to be celebrated for his contributions to modern art. Today, Cézanne is recognized as a key figure in the transition from 19th-century aesthetics to a new and radically different world of art in the 20th century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
